      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{3}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{3},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(1+\\sqrt{6})(\\sqrt{1}0+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{1}0+\\sqrt{3})} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{60}+\\sqrt{18}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{15}+3\\sqrt{2}}{10-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{10}+2\\sqrt{15}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
